  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday through Friday)\nIssued at 345 PM CDT Sat Sep 5 2026\n\nNot much has changed in the Long Term with this forecast update. An \nupper-level trough moving across the northern CONUS will help push \nthe ridge farther to the east early in the week before it migrates \nwestward again during the latter half of the week. Our area stays \nmainly on the periphery of the ridge. As a result, temperatures \nremain above normal through next week, but are still expected to be \nrelatively cooler than what we have seen the past few days. Highs \nlargely top out in the mid-to-upper 90s, with upper 80s and low 90s \nin the higher terrain. Low rain chances (10-30%) will mainly be \nfound in/near the higher terrain south and west of the Pecos \nthroughout the week. Going into next weekend, the forecast remains a \nbit nebulous. The Euro has been fairly consistent in keeping the \nridge nearby during this timeframe (and therefore keeping \ntemperatures warmer), whereas the GFS has shown a bit more variation \nfrom run to run, with most recent solutions depicting a trough \nattempting to push the ridge farther east. In any case, it looks \nlike unseasonably warm conditions will prevail this week, and rain \nchances will largely be limited outside of the higher terrain.\n\nSprang",
